where 𝜆 is the wavelength, n is the refractive index of the medium, and 𝜃 is the semi-angle of the light focused by the optical system. The same formula had been proven by Hermann von Helmholtz in 1874. The portion of the denominator n sin 𝜃 is called the numerical aperture (NA) and can reach about 1.4–1.6 in modern optics, hence the Abbe limit is d = ⁠𝜆/2.8⁠.

{\displaystyle d={\frac {\lambda }{2n\sin {\theta }}}}

