where C is a material-specific Curie constant, T is the absolute temperature, and TC is the Curie temperature, both measured in kelvin. The law predicts a singularity in the susceptibility at T = TC. Below this temperature, the ferromagnet has a spontaneous magnetization. It was developed by Pierre Weiss in 1907, extending Curie's law, named after Pierre Curie.

